  <h2>Jerky Gun Tips (from a Pitmaster)</h2>
  <p>The rest of the internet will tell you the basics. Here's the stuff I only figured out after batches went sideways — the <strong>jerky gun tips</strong> that actually save the day mid-batch:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mix the meat until it turns tacky.</strong> Push it 2 to 3 minutes with your hands. When you can press a clump and it stays together like clay, the proteins are bound and your jerky will hold shape. Skip this step and it crumbles on the tray. This is the single biggest crumbly-jerky fix.</li>
    <li><strong>Rest the mix 1 to 24 hours before extruding.</strong> The cure needs time to work and the flavors need time to marry. Fresh-mixed goes in fine but pales next to rested.</li>
    <li><strong>If the gun spits or blows out mid-strip, stop.</strong> That's an air pocket working its way to the nozzle. Retract the plunger, open the barrel, tamp the meat down again, reload the trapped section, and continue. Don't just squeeze harder — you'll spray meat.</li>
    <li><strong>If the trigger gets hard to pull, chill the whole gun for 10 minutes.</strong> The meat has warmed and smeared. Cold meat flows; warm meat resists.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't crank the retaining ring past finger-tight.</strong> The nozzle only needs to seal, not weld itself on. Overtightened rings are the #1 reason for post-batch cleaning rage.</li>
    <li><strong>Blot the finished jerky.</strong> Even lean jerky throws a few beads of oil on the surface as it cools. A paper-towel wipe now buys you a week of shelf life later.</li>
    <li><strong>Cool completely before bagging.</strong> Warm jerky sealed in plastic sweats, and sweaty jerky grows mold in three days. Ten minutes on a paper towel and you're safe to bag.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Best Jerky Gun Brands Compared</h2>
  <p>There's no single "best" jerky gun — there's the right one for how much you'll actually use it. Here's the honest comparison of the brands that show up on every jerky-gun search: LEM (aka the Jerky Cannon), Nesco, Weston, Hi Mountain, Cabela's, and Presto. I'm not fabricating star ratings or prices — those move around and every list on the internet is out of date the day it publishes. What follows is what's actually different about each:</p>
